wide race"], "rationale": "Arkansas's fifteen-point Republican lean is the dominant evidence, and nothing in the record contradicts it: there are no state polls, no ratings, and no spending or market signals pointing the other way. The open seat strips out incumbency effects without giving either party a reason to expect movement.", "watch": "Any Arkansas-specific presidential polling, a strong national Democratic environment, or signs the Republican nominee runs behind recent GOP margins in the state could tighten the number.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Arkansas sits about fifteen points to the right of the country as a whole, and that partisan lean is the dominant fact in this open presidential race. The incumbent president is term-limited, so neither side carries an incumbency advantage. No public polls of the race, no expert ratings, and no market pricing are available, leaving the state's underlying partisanship to do nearly all of the forecasting work.\n\nRecent coverage centers on down-ballot contests, including a Democratic House bid polling well in the Little Rock-based 2nd District and the 2026 governor's race, rather than the presidential picture. Even a strong Democratic year in one House seat would move a statewide race this lopsided only modestly. The Republican nominee starts with a clear structural advantage, and the main uncertainty is how large it proves on Election Day.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0496, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0891, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0911, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0924, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0837, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem"